#import std.logger;

// `flush` on its own, and what it is worth.
//
// A barrier is a **sink handoff**: it pushes what this program has buffered into
// the stream's implementation and returns. It is not a durability barrier. It
// does not reach a disk, it does not fsync, and it promises nothing about what
// survives a crash on the far side of the handoff. Nothing in the checked model
// claims otherwise, so nothing in the emitted C claims it either — `flush` emits
// exactly one `fflush`.
//
// `flush` names no rendering, so a file that only flushes needs no `std.fmt`
// import; it is `std.logger` that grants the barrier. This program is therefore
// the smallest complete `source.std-io.v1` module there is, and it writes not
// one byte to either stream.
//
// `flush` takes no operand. `flush 1;` refuses at `Q030`, and `total |> flush;`
// refuses at `Q037` — a barrier is not a pipe stage.
fn main() -> unit effects { io } {
    flush;
    return;
}
